% ============================================================================
% fossil-preservation-type — three named STRUCTURES a fossil can be preserved
% as, and what each actually is (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A fossil is not always the organism itself — sometimes it is the SHAPE the
% organism left behind. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? fossil_preservation_type(mold, $D)
%       % three_dimensional_impression_of_all_or_part_of_a_body_fossil_or_trace_fossil
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `fossil_preservation_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a preservation-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the preservation
% type it names:
%
%     ? fossil_preservation_type($T, consists_of_the_evidence_of_living_organisms_but_not_the_actual_organism_itself)
%       % trace_fossil
%
% DISTINCT FROM `fossil-formation-type.adj` (a sibling library in this same
% directory): that table names three FORMATION MECHANISMS (amber, cast_or_mold,
% permineralization) from a different source (Ducksters, consensus), and
% deliberately bundles mold and cast together into ONE coarse row
% (`cast_or_mold`) since its own source only gives that pairing one combined
% sentence. THIS table instead names three PRESERVATION STRUCTURES from a
% more detailed primary source that gives mold and cast their OWN separate
% defining sentences, refining that coarser pairing rather than duplicating
% it — the two tables answer different questions (how did it form? vs. what
% shape is it?) and neither one supersedes the other.
%
% The three preservation types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type            description                                                                       quote (verbatim)
%     --------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     mold             three_dimensional_impression_of_all_or_part_of_a_body_fossil_or_trace_fossil        "A three-dimensional impression of all or part of a body fossil or trace fossil."
%     cast             replica_of_an_organism_or_a_trace_produced_by_the_infilling_of_a_natural_mold        "A replica of an organism or a trace such as a footprint or burrow produced by the infilling of a natural mold."
%     trace_fossil     consists_of_the_evidence_of_living_organisms_but_not_the_actual_organism_itself      "Trace fossils consist of the evidence of living organisms, but not of the actual plant, animal, or microorganism itself."
%
% Provenance: `mold` and `cast` are quoted verbatim from the formal glossary
% section of the National Park Service's "Mold Casts and Steinkerns" article;
% `trace_fossil` is quoted verbatim from that same article's introductory
% paragraph (the page's own glossary list does not carry a separate entry for
% it, but the introduction states it in one clean, complete, standalone
% sentence). `trust authoritative` — the National Park Service is a primary
% U.S. government (.gov) source, the same tier `volcano-type.adj` (a sibling
% library in this directory, USGS-sourced) already uses. WebFetch-verified
% twice — the second pass specifically pulled the page's glossary-vs-prose
% structure and confirmed neither quoted sentence is truncated or bundles in
% an example the way a rejected row would.
%
% Honest abstention on `steinkern`: a real, well-documented term the SAME
% page defines with its own clean sentence ("A stony three-dimensional cast
% of the interior of a shell or other hollow organic structure made by
% sediment infilling prior to the shell being dissolved.") — but the page
% itself frames a steinkern as a SPECIFIC KIND of cast (an internal cast),
% not a fourth preservation type alongside these three. So a recall on it
% ABSTAINS rather than inventing a peer-level row for a sub-category. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table fossil_preservation_type {
    columns type, description

    row (mold, three_dimensional_impression_of_all_or_part_of_a_body_fossil_or_trace_fossil)
    row (cast, replica_of_an_organism_or_a_trace_produced_by_the_infilling_of_a_natural_mold)
    row (trace_fossil, consists_of_the_evidence_of_living_organisms_but_not_the_actual_organism_itself)

    source "A three-dimensional impression of all or part of a body fossil or trace fossil."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/mold-casts-and-steinkerns.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
